course: dictionary
lesson: 1173
title: Broke: one word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Broke» is the past form of break. Today you break something, and yesterday you broke it: «I broke the window.» Here broke tells us something cracked or snapped in the past.
explain: The same word can also mean having no money. «I'm broke until my next pocket money» means I have no money right now. This is informal, so use it in chat or stories, not in a formal letter.
explain: Look at the words around broke to find its meaning. A thing that cracked or stopped working gives the first meaning; a person with no money gives the second. Put your meaning into the sentence to check it.
explain: Broke also appears in phrases. Broke down can mean stopped working, or started crying. Broke out means started suddenly, or escaped. Broke the record means did better than anyone before. Learn these as chunks, not single words.
fact: Broke is the past simple of break: break, breaks, broke, broken, breaking.
fact: «Broke» as an adjective is informal and means having no money.
fact: In «the glass broke into pieces», broke means it cracked into pieces.
fact: In «she broke the record», broke means she did better than the old record.
fact: Standard English says «the window was broken», not «the window got broke»; «got broke» is informal or dialect.
exercise: In «The glass broke into pieces», what does broke mean? | *It cracked or snapped | It won a prize | It was very quiet | It had no money
exercise: «I'm broke until my next pocket money.» What does broke mean here? | *Having no money | Stopped working | Shattered into pieces | Did better than before
exercise: Choose the right form: «The vase had ___ into bits.» | *broken | broke | breaking | breaks
test: In «She broke the record», what does broke show? | Something cracked | She had no money | *She did better than anyone before | She stopped crying
test: Which phrase means stopped working, or started crying? | *broke down | broke the record | broke up | broke out
exam: Which sentence is written in Standard English? | The window got broke | The swings is broke | I am broke about the window | *The window was broken
